-- POST 1: Rise of LLMs
(
  'The Rise of Large Language Models: How GPT Changed Everything',
  'rise-of-large-language-models',
  'An exploration of how transformer-based language models reshaped AI—from the attention mechanism to emergent capabilities and what it all means for practitioners.',
  E'# The Rise of Large Language Models: How GPT Changed Everything\n\nIn November 2022, the world changed. Not with a bang, but with a chat interface.\n\nWhen OpenAI released ChatGPT, it became the fastest-growing consumer application in history — 100 million users in two months. But this was merely the visible tip of an iceberg forming for years in research labs.\n\n## The Transformer Revolution\n\nThe story begins in 2017, with a paper titled *"Attention Is All You Need"* by Vaswani et al. at Google Brain. The transformer architecture solved a fundamental problem: how to efficiently capture long-range dependencies in text.\n\nPrevious approaches — RNNs and LSTMs — processed text sequentially, one word at a time. By the time you reached the end of a long sentence, the model had "forgotten" the beginning.\n\nTransformers solved this with **self-attention**: every word in a sequence can directly attend to every other word, regardless of distance.\n\n```python\ndef attention(Q, K, V, d_k):\n    scores = Q @ K.T / math.sqrt(d_k)\n    weights = softmax(scores)\n    return weights @ V\n```\n\n## From BERT to GPT\n\nTwo training paradigms emerged:\n\n**BERT (2018)** — Bidirectional. Trained by masking random words and predicting them from context. Excellent for understanding tasks: classification, NER, Q&A.\n\n**GPT (2018)** — Autoregressive. Predict the next token given all previous tokens. This simple objective turned out to be extraordinarily powerful.\n\nGPT-3, with 175 billion parameters, demonstrated something genuinely surprising: **emergent capabilities**. Given just 3–4 examples in the prompt, it generalized to new tasks without any weight updates — something traditional ML required thousands of examples for.\n\n| Model | Parameters | Year | Key Capability |\n|-------|-----------|------|----------------|\n| GPT | 117M | 2018 | Language generation |\n| GPT-2 | 1.5B | 2019 | Coherent long-form text |\n| GPT-3 | 175B | 2020 | Few-shot learning |\n| GPT-4 | ~1T est. | 2023 | Multimodal reasoning |\n\n## The Alignment Problem and RLHF\n\nRaw GPT-3 was powerful but unreliable — confident hallucinations, harmful outputs, inconsistent behavior. The breakthrough was **Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF)**:\n\n1. **Supervised Fine-Tuning**: train on human-written example conversations\n2. **Reward Modeling**: train a model to predict human preferences between two responses\n3. **PPO Optimization**: use RL to maximize the reward model score\n\nThis simple addition transformed a text predictor into something that could follow instructions, refuse harmful requests, and maintain consistent personas.\n\n## What This Means for Data Scientists\n\nLLMs fundamentally changed what is possible in NLP:\n\n**Before**: Every task required specialized models, task-specific training data, and deep ML expertise.\n\n**After**: Many NLP tasks are solvable with a well-crafted prompt.\n\nBut this does not make data scientists obsolete — it elevates the work:\n\n- Prompt engineering is a real skill with measurable impact\n- Evaluating LLM outputs rigorously requires domain expertise\n- Fine-tuning for specific domains still yields significant gains\n- RAG systems need solid data engineering\n- Production deployment requires systems thinking\n\n## The Competitive Landscape in 2025\n\nCapability is becoming commoditized. The landscape is rich: Anthropic Claude, Google Gemini, Meta Llama, Mistral. Differentiation now lies in **efficiency**, **safety**, **cost**, and **fine-tuning ease**.\n\nThe next frontier: Mixture of Experts (MoE), chain-of-thought reasoning, agent architectures, and 1M+ token context windows.\n\nThe models of 2025 are not just better than GPT-3. They represent a qualitative shift — and we are still in the early innings.\n\n---\n\n*Jiale Cheng is a Senior Data Scientist working at the intersection of machine learning, data engineering, and real-world AI deployment.*',
  true,
  8
),

-- POST 2: RAG Systems
(
  'Building RAG Systems: Beyond Simple Vector Search',
  'building-rag-systems-beyond-vector-search',
  'A hands-on guide to production-grade Retrieval-Augmented Generation — hybrid search, re-ranking, evaluation frameworks, and the failure modes nobody warns you about.',
  E'# Building RAG Systems: Beyond Simple Vector Search\n\nRetrieval-Augmented Generation has become the dominant paradigm for building LLM applications over proprietary data. But most production systems that start with "just embed everything and do cosine similarity" quickly discover its limits.\n\nThis is a deep dive into what separates naive RAG from production-grade RAG.\n\n## Why RAG? The Context Window Problem\n\nLLMs are frozen in time. Your company''s internal docs, your customer''s support history, your Q4 earnings — none of it is in the model weights.\n\nRAG solves this by retrieval:\n\n```\nQuery → Retrieve relevant chunks → Augment prompt → Generate\n```\n\nSimple in theory. Surprisingly hard in production.\n\n## The Problems with Naive RAG\n\n### 1. Semantic Gap\n\nEmbedding similarity measures semantic closeness, not *relevance to the question*. "What causes revenue decline?" and a chunk containing "Revenue declined 12% in Q3" are semantically distant in embedding space — despite being highly relevant. Keywords matter. Embeddings sometimes miss them.\n\n### 2. Chunking Strategy is Non-Trivial\n\nFixed 512-token chunks split concepts across boundaries, losing context:\n\n```python\n# Bad: arbitrary token splits\ntext_splitter = CharacterTextSplitter(chunk_size=512)\n\n# Better: semantic chunking\ntext_splitter = SemanticChunker(\n    embeddings=embedder,\n    breakpoint_threshold_type="percentile"\n)\n```\n\n### 3. The k Selection Problem\n\nToo few chunks: miss relevant context. Too many: dilute the signal with noise, increasing cost and degrading generation quality.\n\n## Advanced Retrieval: Hybrid Search\n\nThe state of the art combines **sparse** (BM25/keyword) with **dense** (semantic embedding) retrieval:\n\n```python\nclass HybridRetriever:\n    def __init__(self, chunks, embedder, alpha=0.5):\n        self.alpha = alpha  # 0 = pure BM25, 1 = pure semantic\n        tokenized = [c.split() for c in chunks]\n        self.bm25 = BM25Okapi(tokenized)\n        self.embeddings = embedder.embed_documents(chunks)\n\n    def retrieve(self, query, k=10):\n        sparse = self.bm25.get_scores(query.split())\n        q_emb = self.embedder.embed_query(query)\n        dense = cosine_similarity([q_emb], self.embeddings)[0]\n        scores = self.alpha * normalize(dense) + (1 - self.alpha) * normalize(sparse)\n        return top_k(scores, k)\n```\n\nHybrid search consistently outperforms either approach alone by **10–20%** on retrieval benchmarks.\n\n## Re-ranking\n\nInitial retrieval casts a wide net (top-20). A cross-encoder re-ranker applies a more accurate model to re-order the candidates:\n\n```python\nfrom sentence_transformers import CrossEncoder\n\nreranker = CrossEncoder("cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2")\n\ndef rerank(query, candidates, top_k=5):\n    pairs = [(query, c) for c in candidates]\n    scores = reranker.predict(pairs)\n    ranked = sorted(zip(scores, candidates), reverse=True)\n    return [c for _, c in ranked[:top_k]]\n```\n\nCross-encoders see query and document together — far more accurate than embedding similarity, but too slow for first-pass retrieval at scale. The two-stage pattern solves both problems.\n\n## The Evaluation Framework\n\nYou cannot improve what you cannot measure. The RAGAS framework provides standard metrics:\n\n| Metric | Measures |\n|--------|----------|\n| Context Precision | Are retrieved chunks actually relevant? |\n| Context Recall | Are all relevant chunks retrieved? |\n| Faithfulness | Does the answer reflect the context? |\n| Answer Relevancy | Does the answer address the question? |\n\n**Build your evaluation set first.** Before optimizing anything.\n\n## Common Failure Modes\n\n**"Close But No Cigar"** — The right document exists but does not rank in top-k because the query phrasing does not match. Fix: query expansion + higher k + reranking.\n\n**Chunk Context Loss** — A chunk says *"It increased by 23%"* but the antecedent is in the previous chunk. Fix: overlap + parent-child chunking.\n\n**Hallucination on Failure** — When nothing relevant is found, LLMs often make something up. Fix: explicit "I don''t know" grounding in the system prompt + retrieval confidence thresholds.\n\n**Stale Index** — Documents updated in the source system but not re-indexed. Fix: change data capture (CDC) triggers, incremental indexing with version tracking.\n\n## Closing Thoughts\n\nRAG is a system design problem, not a plug-and-play solution. The difference between a demo and production:\n\n- **Evaluation-driven development** — measure before you optimize\n- **Hybrid retrieval** — never rely on embeddings alone\n- **Re-ranking** — cast a wide net, then be selective\n- **Explicit failure handling** — defined behavior when retrieval fails\n\n---\n\n*Lessons from building RAG systems across financial services and enterprise software contexts.*',
  true,
  10
),

-- POST 3: MLOps
(
  'MLOps in Practice: Shipping Models That Actually Stay Working',
  'mlops-in-practice-shipping-models',
  'Hard-won lessons from deploying ML models in financial services — monitoring, drift detection, feature pipelines, and why the model is never really the hard part.',
  E'# MLOps in Practice: Shipping Models That Actually Stay Working\n\nEveryone talks about building models. Few talk about keeping them working six months later.\n\nAfter shipping ML systems in financial services — where a degraded model is not just a performance issue but a regulatory one — I have developed strong opinions about what MLOps actually requires in practice. This is not about the tools. It is about the problems the tools exist to solve.\n\n## The Dirty Secret: The Model Is Not the Hard Part\n\nWhen a production ML system fails, the cause is almost never the model architecture. It is:\n\n- A feature pipeline that started returning nulls\n- A data schema change upstream that nobody announced\n- Training–serving skew: the model sees different data at inference than it trained on\n- A business rule change that invalidated the assumptions baked into the labels\n- Gradual data drift that eroded performance over 3 months until someone noticed\n\nThe model is a function. The infrastructure around it is the product. **MLOps is software engineering applied to systems that learn from data** — and all the standard software engineering problems still apply, plus new ones.\n\n## The ML System Stack\n\nA production ML system has more moving parts than most engineers expect:\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│               Business Logic                │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│          Model Serving / Inference          │\n├───────────────────┬─────────────────────────┤\n│   Model Registry  │   Feature Store         │\n├───────────────────┴─────────────────────────┤\n│        Training Pipeline (Scheduled)        │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│         Feature Engineering Pipeline        │\n├─────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│              Data Sources / ETL             │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\nEach layer is a potential failure point. Monitoring only the model layer — the top — is like monitoring a building''s roof while ignoring the foundation.\n\n## Feature Pipelines: Where Most Failures Live\n\nA model is only as good as its features. Feature pipelines are where production ML systems most often silently degrade.\n\nThe core problem: **training–serving skew**.\n\n```python\n# Training time — you compute features over historical data\ndf["days_since_last_transaction"] = (\n    df["transaction_date"] - df["last_transaction_date"]\n).dt.days\n\n# Serving time — you compute the same feature in real time\n# BUT: what is "now"? What timezone? What if last_transaction is NULL?\n# What if the data schema changed and the field is named differently?\ndef compute_days_since_last_tx(customer_id: str) -> float:\n    last_tx = db.query("SELECT MAX(date) FROM transactions WHERE customer_id = ?", customer_id)\n    if last_tx is None:\n        return -1  # <-- Training used NaN, not -1. Model behavior undefined.\n    return (datetime.now() - last_tx).days\n```\n\nThe fix is not just code review. It is **a feature store** — a system where the same computation is registered once and used both at training time (point-in-time correct historical values) and serving time (real-time values from the same logic).\n\nIn practice: Feast, Tecton, or — for simpler setups — a well-structured dbt project + a Redis cache.\n\n## Monitoring: What to Actually Measure\n\nMost teams set up model monitoring and measure accuracy. Then they discover the ground truth label arrives 30 days after the prediction, so their accuracy metric is always a month stale and useless for catching live issues.\n\nThe monitoring stack should have three layers:\n\n**1. Data quality monitoring** (leading indicator — available immediately)\n```python\n# Schema validation\npandera_schema.validate(incoming_features)\n\n# Distribution checks (Great Expectations / custom)\nassert df["credit_score"].between(300, 850).all(), "Credit score out of expected range"\nassert df["age"].notna().mean() > 0.99, "More than 1% null ages — pipeline issue suspected"\n```\n\n**2. Model input drift** (leading indicator — available immediately)\n\nPopulation Stability Index (PSI) is the industry standard for detecting feature drift:\n\n```python\ndef psi(expected, actual, bins=10):\n    breakpoints = np.linspace(0, 1, bins + 1)\n    e = np.histogram(expected, breakpoints)[0] / len(expected)\n    a = np.histogram(actual, breakpoints)[0] / len(actual)\n    e = np.where(e == 0, 1e-4, e)\n    a = np.where(a == 0, 1e-4, a)\n    return np.sum((a - e) * np.log(a / e))\n\n# PSI < 0.1: no significant change\n# PSI 0.1–0.25: moderate change, investigate\n# PSI > 0.25: major shift, consider retraining\n```\n\n**3. Outcome monitoring** (lagging indicator — available when labels arrive)\n\nPrecision, recall, F1, AUC — standard metrics, but tracked over time with alerting thresholds.\n\n## Retraining Strategy\n\nTwo approaches, often confused:\n\n**Scheduled retraining** — retrain on a fixed cadence (weekly, monthly). Simple. Does not adapt to sudden shifts. Appropriate for stable domains.\n\n**Triggered retraining** — retrain when drift metrics cross a threshold. More complex but responsive. Required in volatile environments (financial markets, fraud patterns).\n\nIn practice: start with scheduled. Add drift-triggered retraining only when you have evidence that scheduled is insufficient.\n\n```python\n# Simplified trigger logic\nif psi_score > 0.25 or performance_drop > 0.05:\n    trigger_retraining_pipeline()\n    alert_team("Model drift detected — retraining triggered")\n```\n\n## Experiment Tracking and Model Registry\n\nWithout experiment tracking, you cannot answer basic questions:\n\n- What exact data was this model trained on?\n- What hyperparameters produced this result?\n- Which model version is currently in production?\n- Can we roll back to the previous version?\n\nMLflow is the standard open-source solution:\n\n```python\nimport mlflow\n\nwith mlflow.start_run():\n    mlflow.log_params({"n_estimators": 500, "max_depth": 6, "learning_rate": 0.05})\n    model = train_model(X_train, y_train, params)\n    metrics = evaluate(model, X_val, y_val)\n    mlflow.log_metrics(metrics)\n    mlflow.sklearn.log_model(model, "model", registered_model_name="fraud_detector")\n```\n\nThe model registry gives you:\n- Versioned model artifacts with metadata\n- Stage transitions (Staging → Production → Archived)\n- Audit trail for compliance\n\n## The Deployment Patterns\n\n**Shadow mode**: new model runs in parallel with production, predictions are logged but not served. Zero risk, full observability. Use before any significant model change.\n\n**Canary deployment**: route 5% of traffic to the new model, monitor metrics, gradually increase. Standard for high-stakes applications.\n\n**A/B testing**: split traffic between models with statistical significance testing. Requires enough volume to detect meaningful differences.\n\nIn financial services: shadow mode first, always. No exceptions.\n\n## What I Have Learned\n\nAfter deploying models in AML detection, insurance risk, and pharmaceutical market intelligence:\n\n1. **Define "working" before deployment.** Latency SLAs, accuracy thresholds, drift alert levels. Write them down. Get sign-off. Otherwise "is the model working?" has no answer.\n\n2. **Treat feature pipelines like production services.** SLAs, alerting, ownership. Not data science side projects.\n\n3. **Ground truth is a product.** Someone has to own the labeling process. If labels arrive inconsistently, your lagging metrics are noise.\n\n4. **The model is replaceable. The pipeline is not.** A well-engineered feature pipeline and monitoring stack can swap models underneath without touching production code.\n\n5. **Documentation decays. Tests do not.** Write tests for your feature transformations. They will catch the upstream schema change before it reaches production.\n\nThe gap between a notebook model and a production ML system is not technical sophistication — it is engineering discipline applied consistently.\n\n---\n\n*Jiale Cheng is a Senior Data Scientist who has shipped ML systems in financial crime detection, insurance risk, and enterprise automation.*',
  true,
  11
)

ON CONFLICT (slug) DO UPDATE SET
  title        = EXCLUDED.title,
  summary      = EXCLUDED.summary,
  content      = EXCLUDED.content,
  published    = EXCLUDED.published,
  reading_time = EXCLUDED.reading_time;

SELECT slug, title, published, reading_time
FROM portfolio.posts
ORDER BY created_at;
